  "architecture": "H1 changes only the duplicate-name validation operation in TokenCursor.parseStart. For rawCount < 16, stop computing/storing per-character FNV hashes and compare candidate spans directly (length/span equality); when the wide-table path is entered, compute hashes for table insertion/lookup. H1B is a contingent comparator prefilter (first character/last character/length before full span compare), not a cache, classifier, semantic transport, or namespace mechanism. Neither candidate requires new persistent state or allocation; H1 adds work only at the wide transition, while H1B adds branches and may hurt short-name cases.",
  "report": "Candidate matrix:\n\n| Candidate | Target op | Cost / structure | Closest prior | Structural difference | State / lookup / branch / allocation added | Operations removed | Benefit bound |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 | TokenCursor.parseStart duplicate-name checks for rawCount < DUPLICATE_TABLE_THRESHOLD (16) | Exact length/span duplicate comparisons for narrow tags; deferred FNV hash generation until wide-table transition | Baseline TokenCursor at 9a839ffb; commit 015f15b7 linearized entity validation but did not change duplicate-name hashing | Hashing policy depends on duplicate-table width: narrow path is hash-free, wide path remains hashed | No persistent state, lookup, or allocation; one width transition branch already exists | Per-character `Math.imul`/FNV updates for every narrow attribute name; hash array writes on narrow path (if implementation defers storage) | Unknown until counters and workload profiles; theoretical ceiling is only the removed narrow-name hash work, not whole-parser throughput |\n| H1B | Same narrow duplicate comparison loop | Add first/last/length prefilter before exact span compare | Existing `spansEqual` already performs exact comparison after hash; prior dossiers caution against common hot-loop branches | Comparator prefilter, not hash removal and not a semantic cache | No state/allocation; adds up to three comparisons/branches per candidate and possibly extra boundary reads | Only characters traversed after a failed prefilter (if any); does not remove FNV unless combined with H1 | Unknown and blocker-contingent; do not claim value unless direct span comparison is measured as the blocker |\n\nNovelty conclusion: the exact H1/H1B mechanism was not found in the fetched origin/archive index, archived entity experiment, origin/evidence tree at cd3202d7d6664528e1be2427fcc32e5763ceef06, baseline report tree, or the examined TokenCursor commit-history results. This is a scoped negative search, not proof of global novelty. The archive explicitly says the research ZIP (`inputs/stax-purejs-new-frontier-research.zip`) was unavailable; therefore ZIP kernels/check/retention coverage is unknown. The archive's separate defect is evidence of the target area, not prior art for H1: wide repeated elements can fail with `Duplicate attribute: a15` (namespace fixture `a12`) because reusable duplicate-table generation is not advanced. Do not classify that defect as cache work, classifier work, semantic transport, or an H1 result.\n\nReusable latest-archive statistical/profile/workload paths: `packages/benchmark/statistical-analysis.mjs`, `profile-parsers.mjs`, `profile-simple.mjs`, `iterable-attr-materialization.mjs`, `event-attributes-container.mjs`, `runtime-stax-runner.mjs`, and `common/parser-scenarios.mjs`. Archived entity replay is `experiments/2026-09-13-entity-single-eval/harness/packages/benchmark/entity-single-eval/analyze.mjs`; it is offline-only and does not establish H1 headroom. No validation, build, test, formatter, or lint command was run."
}

